@@ -0,0 +1,66 @@
+# PlacementRules spec cleanup
+
+`src/ServerScriptService/Tests/Specs/PlacementRules.spec.luau` now tests only the contract that
+`PlacementRules.luau` documents: `snap`, plus every acceptance rule listed on `validate`.
+`PlacementRules.luau` is unchanged.
+
+## The two failing tests
+
+- **"rejects a position that isn't a Vector3"** called the matchers with a colon
+  (`expect(ok):toBe(false)`). Jest Lua matchers use a dot, so the colon passed the expectation
+  object in as the expected value, and the test could never pass. Fixed to use dots.
+- **"doesn't mark the cell occupied"** counted the table's entries and then checked
+  `expect(count).toBeFalsy()`. In Lua `0` is truthy, so this failed even though `validate`
+  correctly left the table alone. It now checks `expect(next(occupied)).toBeNil()`.
+
+## Removed
+
+| Test | Why |
+|---|---|
+| "snaps positions to cell centers" | It copied the `snapAxis` formula into the test, so it checked the code against itself. The table of hand-written examples already covers snapping. |
+| `_isFinite` (3 tests) | They tested a private helper. Renaming or inlining it broke the suite even though placement still worked. NaN and ±infinity are now tested through `validate`, which is the documented rule. |
+| "calls snap exactly once" | It spied on how `validate` works inside, not on what it returns. Any refactor (calling a local snap, caching) broke it. |
+| "rejects placing twice in the same cell" | It only passed because the test before it wrote into a shared module-level `occupied` table. It duplicated the occupied-cell test and broke if tests ran in another order or one at a time. |
+| "BlockTypes has the right block types" | It pinned colors, materials and order in `BlockTypes`, which aren't placement rules. It broke on any palette tweak. If BlockTypes needs a spec, it belongs in its own file. |
+
+## Changed
+
+- **No shared state.** The module-level `occupied` table is gone, and every test builds its own.
+- **One input changed per rejection.** Every rejection test starts from a single known-good
+  request (Brick at `(1, 1, 1)`, root 8 studs away) and changes one input. The baseline is
+  checked by "accepts a valid request…", so a rejection can only come from the rule under test.
+- **Reason text is no longer checked.** The docs only promise "a short reason for the server
+  log", so rejection tests check `ok == false` and that a string reason came back. Rewording a log
+  message no longer breaks the suite. *Assumption:* the reason strings are for logging only and
+  nothing parses them.
+- **Height cases match real cell centers.** Cell centers are always 2 + 4k, so the edge cases are
+  now the cells at Y = -2 (rejected), Y = 2 (resting on the Baseplate), Y = 198 (highest under
+  `MaxHeight`) and Y = 202 (rejected). The old rows tested the same thing, but their labels
+  ("Y = 200") made it look like the raw position was being checked.
+
+## Added (rules that weren't covered)
+
+- `rootPosition == nil` is rejected ("the player has a character").
+- Infinite positions (`math.huge`, `-math.huge`) are rejected. Before, only NaN was tested.
+- `blockTypeName` values that aren't strings (`nil`, a number) are rejected, and so are `position`
+  values that are `nil` or a string.
+- Reach is measured to the cell's center, not the raw position. A point 25.9 studs away whose
+  cell is exactly 24 away is accepted.
+- The neighboring cell is accepted while one cell is occupied, so the occupied check doesn't
+  reject everything.
+
+The suite went from 31 to 35 tests. Most are cheap `it.each` rows, and every rule in the
+`validate` doc comment now has at least one accept case and one reject case.
+
+## Assumptions
+
+- The expected numbers assume `BlockSize = 4`, `MaxHeight = 200` and `MaxReach = 24`. That's
+  written down at the top of the spec. Changing those values is a change to the rules, not a
+  refactor, so updating the specs then is expected.
+- The docs say "above the Baseplate (Y > 0)" but the code checks `center.Y < 0`. No cell center can
+  be exactly 0, so the two behave the same and the spec doesn't try to tell them apart.
+
+## Verification
+
+Specs can't run in this environment. On the changed spec, `luau-lsp analyze`, `selene src` and
+`stylua --check src` all pass (see the README's Checks section).
